h the radius changes as the volume changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337532} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d3818b-f99d-5a24-b5f8-6df2128b8b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the radius changes as the surface area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4fb944c-c55d-5347-b4f6-7c78b85fb0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337532} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62051ba-1ffa-5ae8-a7ea-8260e883f62d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5325b1f7-e675-515b-98cd-c46e0c3ccc93" :question/math-academy-id "q-72099" :question/problem "Consider a sphere such that its volume $V$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${r}^{′}(V)$ if $r$ is the length of the radius of the sphere?" :question/worked-solution "The function $r(V)$ gives the length of the radius of a sphere with volume $V.$ So,\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(V)=\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}V}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }r}{\\text{Change in }V}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${r}^{′}(V)$ is the rate at which the radius changes as the volume changes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f73173-53b3-594c-9f00-73f6211f3aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336106} {:answer/id #uuid "2f86ab33-c24f-513c-9f78-18726fd0321b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336488} {:answer/id #uuid "8526b1a3-6e9c-580f-9c0b-82c5736c43b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the perimeter changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337231} {:answer/id #uuid "95ba1001-8463-59e2-bed5-38495c9afaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337400} {:answer/id #uuid "eddb83ed-12b2-59b9-8dd1-0e4ed245941a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f73173-53b3-594c-9f00-73f6211f3aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336106} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa385052-bba0-5f25-abff-7baf2aaa746f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338590}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c47d8541-4bf8-5eff-901b-cba03fe2cd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-72104" :question/problem "Consider a regular pentagon such that the length $s$ of each side varies. What is the best interpretation of ${P}^{′}(s)$ if $P$ is the perimeter of the pentagon?" :question/worked-solution "The function $P(s)$ gives the perimeter of a pentagon with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }P}{\\text{Change in }s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}^{′}(s)$ is the rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c576c65-7b33-57e7-970c-b9e456a33e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the circumference changes as the diameter changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517242} {:answer/id #uuid "56768790-6ef7-53d7-931d-003b578d2afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The diameter of the circle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517261} {:answer/id #uuid "63220a44-b9c9-